#!/bin/sh
# =====================================================
# Fast local feedback before a push. NOT enforcement.
# =====================================================
# This is the half of the 2026-09-03 guard that runs on your machine, and it is
# worth being exact about how little it guarantees:
#
#   * It does not survive a clone. Hooks are not tracked content — this file is
#     tracked, but git will not run it until somebody points git at it:
#
#         git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
#     A fresh clone runs no hook at all until that line is typed. That single
#     manual step is the reason this cannot be the enforcement.
#
#   * `git push --no-verify` skips it, silently and by design.
#
# The enforcement is `.github/workflows/test.yml`, which runs on the server
# where the person who forgot cannot reach it. This exists so you find out in
# the seconds before a push rather than the minutes after one — which for a
# personal name, an absolute path or a photograph's metadata is the difference
# between a local fix and a history rewrite.
#
# To skip it deliberately, use --no-verify and know that CI will still see it.
#
# HOW MUCH IT RUNS, AND THE RULE FOR CHANGING THAT
#
#   The whole suite, because here it takes about seventeen seconds. **The
#   governing constraint is the clock, not the coverage**: a hook slow enough to
#   be resented is a hook that gets `--no-verify`d out of habit, and then it
#   protects nothing at all.
#
#   So the rule is — run everything while everything is fast; when it stops
#   being fast, drop to the disclosure guards alone (`no-personal-names.mjs` and
#   `no-process-records.mjs`), because those are the ones whose findings a push
#   makes irreversible. Everything else CI will redo anyway.
#
# THIS FILE IS CANONICAL for both repositories (supplement 2, item C). The
# Student Submission copy adopts it and adjusts the two commands below under
# exactly the rule above: its suite runs registration and capture work measured
# in minutes, so it runs the two guards rather than `npm test`. That is the rule
# being applied, not a divergence from it.

set -e

printf '\n[pre-push] tsc\n'
npx tsc --noEmit

printf '\n[pre-push] npm test\n'
npm test

printf '\n[pre-push] ok\n'
